From 8752ce36bb33ee0a982d937d4b4dc527d72aa048 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 10:05:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 088/119] scene-referred S6: the tap, and the pixels leaving
 the GPU without stopping it

The value the spec's chain calls the export point exists only as a shader temporary: after S4 the
whole display chain - white balance, ACEScct, CDL, LMT, ODT - is evaluated per pixel inside
HDRFinalPass, so there is no render target holding it. This adds one.

HDRFinalPass grows a second render target under %_RT_SAMPLE2 (the bit S4 freed when it deleted the
dead vignette gate). FilmMapping hands the graded value back out through an out parameter, taken
between the LMT fetch and the ODT fetch and converted back to LINEAR ACEScg on the way. The
inverse shaper is not an operator being undone: the ACEScct encode belongs to the ODT LUT's input
domain, not to the grade, and an EXR of the log value would be a file no ACES tool could read
without being told about our private shaper. The round trip that proves S4 needs scene-linear.

A second mode taps $HDRTarget as it enters the tone map - exposed, ACEScg, ungraded, unvignetted,
un-composited. That is the camera NEGATIVE, and it is a different artefact from the graded frame
rather than a variant of it: the graded tap is the acceptance instrument (it is the only one for
which "apply the same ODT elsewhere and compare" tests the output transform instead of every
operator in front of it), and the negative is what a colourist actually wants.

The readback is the two-phase, fence-carrying kind, over a ring of three FT_STAGE_READBACK
targets: issue the download after the pass, poll a later frame, never block. The one-shot overload
the screenshot path uses Maps immediately and stalls the pipeline - tolerable for a screenshot, not
for 15.8 MiB (1080p) or 63.3 MiB (4K) every frame. A ring of one would mean either a stall or a
sequence with holes; three is what makes "frame N+1's copy is never waiting on frame N's map" hold
at a frame rate that varies. The render thread copies the mapped rows out (they are PITCHED, and
assuming width * 8 is what produces a sheared image) and a low-priority job de-interleaves,
optionally converts AP1 -> AP0, compresses and writes.

Backpressure is honest: one writer job per ring slot, so the ring bounds the queue. If the ring
comes round to a slot whose download has not returned or whose writer is still going, the capture
STOPS and says why. It never drops a frame - a hole in a sequence is invisible until someone tries
to conform it, which is exactly when it costs the most.

Everything is created on arm and released on disarm, and both the tap and the encoding are latched
at that moment rather than read per frame: a sequence whose second half means something different
from its first is a folder of files that look alike and are not.

The stock path is untouched. capture_frames, RT_ReadTexture, SCaptureFormatInfo and
ICaptureFrameListener are all left exactly as they were, so the byte-identity harness that reads
TGA out of that code stays valid. With no capture armed the pass binds one render target and the
shader declares one output, as before. Adding an output to HDRFinalPass does invalidate the shader
permutation cache once, stock variants included - S4 already did that when it removed SAMPLE2.

